# Factory Pattern in Rust

The Factory pattern encapsulates object creation logic, letting you produce instances without exposing the exact construction details to the caller.

Rust doesn't have classical OOP inheritance, so the pattern maps onto **traits + factory functions/methods** that return trait objects or enum variants.

## Practical Example: Notification System

```rust
// The "product" trait — what all notifications can do
trait Notification {
    fn send(&self, recipient: &str, message: &str);
}

// Concrete implementations
struct EmailNotification;
struct SmsNotification;
struct PushNotification;

impl Notification for EmailNotification {
    fn send(&self, recipient: &str, message: &str) {
        println!("[EMAIL] To: {} | {}", recipient, message);
    }
}

impl Notification for SmsNotification {
    fn send(&self, recipient: &str, message: &str) {
        println!("[SMS] To: {} | {}", recipient, message);
    }
}

impl Notification for PushNotification {
    fn send(&self, recipient: &str, message: &str) {
        println!("[PUSH] To: {} | {}", recipient, message);
    }
}

// The factory — single point of creation
enum Channel {
    Email,
    Sms,
    Push,
}

fn create_notification(channel: Channel) -> Box<dyn Notification> {
    match channel {
        Channel::Email => Box::new(EmailNotification),
        Channel::Sms => Box::new(SmsNotification),
        Channel::Push => Box::new(PushNotification),
    }
}

// Usage
fn main() {
    let channels = vec![Channel::Email, Channel::Sms, Channel::Push];

    for ch in channels {
        let notifier = create_notification(ch);
        notifier.send("user@example.com", "Your order shipped");
    }
}
```

## Why this works well in Rust

1. **`Box<dyn Notification>`** — dynamic dispatch gives you runtime polymorphism without inheritance hierarchies.
2. **Exhaustive `match`** — the compiler forces you to handle every variant. Add a new channel and you get a compile error everywhere it's unhandled.
3. **No heap allocation needed?** — if variants are known at compile time, you can use an enum with methods instead of trait objects, avoiding the vtable overhead entirely.

## When to use it

- You have multiple types behind a common interface and the choice depends on runtime config/input.
- You want to isolate construction logic (e.g., each variant needs different setup, credentials, connection pools).
- You're building a plugin-like system where new implementations can be added without touching consumer code.

## Enum-based alternative (zero-cost)

```rust
enum Notifier {
    Email,
    Sms,
    Push,
}

impl Notifier {
    fn send(&self, recipient: &str, message: &str) {
        match self {
            Self::Email => println!("[EMAIL] To: {} | {}", recipient, message),
            Self::Sms   => println!("[SMS] To: {} | {}", recipient, message),
            Self::Push  => println!("[PUSH] To: {} | {}", recipient, message),
        }
    }
}
```

This is simpler and has no dynamic dispatch cost, but it's less extensible — adding a variant means modifying the enum itself. Pick based on whether you need open or closed extensibility.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <variant>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <memory>
#include <sstream>
#include <cctype>

// Forward declaration
struct JsonValue;

using JsonNull = std::nullptr_t;
using JsonBool = bool;
using JsonNumber = double;
using JsonString = std::string;
using JsonArray = std::vector<JsonValue>;
using JsonObject = std::unordered_map<std::string, JsonValue>;

struct JsonValue {
    std::variant<JsonNull, JsonBool, JsonNumber, JsonString, JsonArray, JsonObject> data;

    JsonValue() : data(nullptr) {}
    JsonValue(std::nullptr_t) : data(nullptr) {}
    JsonValue(bool b) : data(b) {}
    JsonValue(double n) : data(n) {}
    JsonValue(const std::string& s) : data(s) {}
    JsonValue(std::string&& s) : data(std::move(s)) {}
    JsonValue(const JsonArray& a) : data(a) {}
    JsonValue(JsonArray&& a) : data(std::move(a)) {}
    JsonValue(const JsonObject& o) : data(o) {}
    JsonValue(JsonObject&& o) : data(std::move(o)) {}

    bool is_null() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonNull>(data); }
    bool is_bool() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonBool>(data); }
    bool is_number() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonNumber>(data); }
    bool is_string() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonString>(data); }
    bool is_array() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonArray>(data); }
    bool is_object() const { return std::holds_alternative<JsonObject>(data); }

    template<typename T>
    const T& get() const { return std::get<T>(data); }
};

class JsonParser {
public:
    static JsonValue parse(const std::string& input) {
        JsonParser parser(input);
        parser.skip_whitespace();
        if (parser.pos_ >= parser.input_.size()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Empty input");
        }
        JsonValue result = parser.parse_value();
        parser.skip_whitespace();
        if (parser.pos_ < parser.input_.size()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Trailing content at position " + std::to_string(parser.pos_));
        }
        return result;
    }

private:
    std::string input_;
    size_t pos_ = 0;

    explicit JsonParser(const std::string& input) : input_(input) {}

    char peek() const {
        if (pos_ >= input_.size()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected end of input");
        }
        return input_[pos_];
    }

    char advance() {
        char c = peek();
        ++pos_;
        return c;
    }

    void expect(char c) {
        char got = advance();
        if (got != c) {
            throw std::runtime_error(
                std::string("Expected '") + c + "' but got '" + got +
                "' at position " + std::to_string(pos_ - 1));
        }
    }

    void skip_whitespace() {
        while (pos_ < input_.size() &&
               (input_[pos_] == ' ' || input_[pos_] == '\t' ||
                input_[pos_] == '\n' || input_[pos_] == '\r')) {
            ++pos_;
        }
    }

    JsonValue parse_value() {
        skip_whitespace();
        char c = peek();
        switch (c) {
            case '"': return parse_string_value();
            case '{': return parse_object();
            case '[': return parse_array();
            case 't': case 'f': return parse_bool();
            case 'n': return parse_null();
            default:
                if (c == '-' || std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(c))) {
                    return parse_number();
                }
                throw std::runtime_error(
                    std::string("Unexpected character '") + c +
                    "' at position " + std::to_string(pos_));
        }
    }

    JsonValue parse_string_value() {
        return JsonValue(parse_string());
    }

    std::string parse_string() {
        expect('"');
        std::string result;
        while (true) {
            if (pos_ >= input_.size()) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Unterminated string");
            }
            char c = advance();
            if (c == '"') {
                return result;
            }
            if (c == '\\') {
                if (pos_ >= input_.size()) {
                    throw std::runtime_error("Unterminated escape sequence");
                }
                char escaped = advance();
                switch (escaped) {
                    case '"':  result += '"'; break;
                    case '\\': result += '\\'; break;
                    case '/':  result += '/'; break;
                    case 'b':  result += '\b'; break;
                    case 'f':  result += '\f'; break;
                    case 'n':  result += '\n'; break;
                    case 'r':  result += '\r'; break;
                    case 't':  result += '\t'; break;
                    case 'u':  result += parse_unicode_escape(); break;
                    default:
                        throw std::runtime_error(
                            std::string("Invalid escape sequence '\\") + escaped + "'");
                }
            } else if (static_cast<unsigned char>(c) < 0x20) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Unescaped control character in string");
            } else {
                result += c;
            }
        }
    }

    std::string parse_unicode_escape() {
        uint32_t codepoint = parse_hex4();

        // Handle UTF-16 surrogate pairs
        if (codepoint >= 0xD800 && codepoint <= 0xDBFF) {
            if (pos_ + 1 >= input_.size() || input_[pos_] != '\\' || input_[pos_ + 1] != 'u') {
                throw std::runtime_error("Expected low surrogate after high surrogate");
            }
            pos_ += 2; // skip \u
            uint32_t low = parse_hex4();
            if (low < 0xDC00 || low > 0xDFFF) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Invalid low surrogate");
            }
            codepoint = 0x10000 + ((codepoint - 0xD800) << 10) + (low - 0xDC00);
        } else if (codepoint >= 0xDC00 && codepoint <= 0xDFFF) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Unexpected low surrogate");
        }

        return codepoint_to_utf8(codepoint);
    }

    uint32_t parse_hex4() {
        if (pos_ + 4 > input_.size()) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Incomplete unicode escape");
        }
        uint32_t result = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
            char c = advance();
            result <<= 4;
            if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') result |= (c - '0');
            else if (c >= 'a' && c <= 'f') result |= (c - 'a' + 10);
            else if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'F') result |= (c - 'A' + 10);
            else throw std::runtime_error(std::string("Invalid hex digit '") + c + "'");
        }
        return result;
    }

    static std::string codepoint_to_utf8(uint32_t cp) {
        std::string result;
        if (cp <= 0x7F) {
            result += static_cast<char>(cp);
        } else if (cp <= 0x7FF) {
            result += static_cast<char>(0xC0 | (cp >> 6));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));
        } else if (cp <= 0xFFFF) {
            result += static_cast<char>(0xE0 | (cp >> 12));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));
        } else if (cp <= 0x10FFFF) {
            result += static_cast<char>(0xF0 | (cp >> 18));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3F));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3F));
            result += static_cast<char>(0x80 | (cp & 0x3F));
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid Unicode codepoint");
        }
        return result;
    }

    JsonValue parse_number() {
        size_t start = pos_;

        if (peek() == '-') advance();

        if (peek() == '0') {
            advance();
            // After leading 0, next char must not be a digit
            if (pos_ < input_.size() && std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Leading zeros not allowed");
            }
        } else if (std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(peek()))) {
            while (pos_ < input_.size() && std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                advance();
            }
        } else {
            throw std::runtime_error("Invalid number");
        }

        // Fractional part
        if (pos_ < input_.size() && input_[pos_] == '.') {
            advance();
            if (pos_ >= input_.size() || !std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Expected digit after decimal point");
            }
            while (pos_ < input_.size() && std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                advance();
            }
        }

        // Exponent
        if (pos_ < input_.size() && (input_[pos_] == 'e' || input_[pos_] == 'E')) {
            advance();
            if (pos_ < input_.size() && (input_[pos_] == '+' || input_[pos_] == '-')) {
                advance();
            }
            if (pos_ >= input_.size() || !std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                throw std::runtime_error("Expected digit in exponent");
            }
            while (pos_ < input_.size() && std::isdigit(static_cast<unsigned char>(input_[pos_]))) {
                advance();
            }
        }

        std::string num_str = input_.substr(start, pos_ - start);
        try {
            return JsonValue(std::stod(num_str));
        } catch (...) {
            throw std::runtime_error("Failed to parse number: " + num_str);
        }
    }

    JsonValue parse_bool() {
        if (input_.compare(pos_, 4, "true") == 0) {
            pos_ += 4;
            return JsonValue(true);
        }
        if (input_.compare(pos_, 5, "false") == 0) {
            pos_ += 5;
            return JsonValue(false);
        }
        throw std::runtime_error("Invalid literal at position " + std::to_string(pos_));
    }

    JsonValue parse_null() {
        if (input_.compare(pos_, 4, "null") == 0) {
            pos_ += 4;
            return JsonValue(nullptr);
        }
        throw std::runtime_error("Invalid literal at position " + std::to_string(pos_));
    }

    JsonValue parse_array() {
        expect('[');
        JsonArray arr;
        skip_whitespace();
        if (pos_ < input_.size() && peek() == ']') {
            advance();
            return JsonValue(std::move(arr));
        }
        while (true) {
            arr.push_back(parse_value());
            skip_whitespace();
            char c = advance();
            if (c == ']') return JsonValue(std::move(arr));
            if (c != ',') {
                throw std::runtime_error(
                    "Expected ',' or ']' in array at position " + std::to_string(pos_ - 1));
            }
        }
    }

    JsonValue parse_object() {
        expect('{');
        JsonObject obj;
        skip_whitespace();
        if (pos_ < input_.size() && peek() == '}') {
            advance();
            return JsonValue(std::move(obj));
        }
        while (true) {
            skip_whitespace();
            if (peek() != '"') {
                throw std::runtime_error("Expected string key in object");
            }
            std::string key = parse_string();
            skip_whitespace();
            expect(':');
            JsonValue value = parse_value();
            obj[std::move(key)] = std::move(value);
            skip_whitespace();
            char c = advance();
            if (c == '}') return JsonValue(std::move(obj));
            if (c != ',') {
                throw std::runtime_error(
                    "Expected ',' or '}' in object at position " + std::to_string(pos_ - 1));
            }
        }
    }
};
```

Edge cases handled:

- **Unicode escapes** including UTF-16 surrogate pairs (`\uD83D\uDE00` → 😀)
- **All escape sequences** (`\n`, `\t`, `\\`, `\"`, `\/`, `\b`, `\f`, `\uXXXX`)
- **Leading zeros** rejected per JSON spec (`007` is invalid)
- **Trailing content** after valid JSON is an error
- **Empty input** throws
- **Unterminated strings** throws
- **Control characters** in strings rejected
- **Deeply nested structures** (limited only by stack depth)
- **Duplicate keys** — last value wins (standard `unordered_map` behavior)
- **Exponent notation** with optional sign (`1.5e-10`)
- **Negative numbers** and bare zero
- **Trailing commas** rejected (strict JSON)

Usage:

```cpp
JsonValue val = JsonParser::parse(R"({"name": "test", "nested": {"arr": [1, null, true]}})");
const auto& obj = val.get<JsonObject>();
const auto& nested = obj.at("nested").get<JsonObject>();
const auto& arr = nested.at("arr").get<JsonArray>();
// arr[0] is 1.0, arr[1] is null, arr[2] is true
```

